The active reference is client/index.html, client/src/main.js, client/shared,
and server/index.mjs plus server/src. The older client/main.js is not the live
entry point.

Aethyrforge adapts the source controller, class definitions, state machines,
following camera, NPC behavior, health, inventory, chat, proximity queries,
terrain/scenery generation, UI layout, lighting/fog, and particle profiles.
Relevant source files include player-entity.js, player-state.js, player-input.js,
npc-entity.js, third-person-camera.js, health-component.js, health-bar.js,
network-controller.js, network-entity-controller.js, inventory-controller.js,
ui-controller.js, floating-name.js, scenery-controller.js, threejs_component.js,
render-component.js, blood-effect.js, sorceror-effect.js, particle-system.js,
level-up-component.js, base.css, and index.html; shared definitions, terrain,
noise, spatial-grid, and spline modules; and the server's world, entity, client,
and login-queue modules.

Owned Rust modules implement authoritative state and the browser prediction
kernel. The browser uses React/Effect UI and direct WebGPU rendering, without a
Three.js or Socket.IO runtime. Fixed 60 Hz simulation and 10 Hz snapshots replace
the source transport. Sequenced controls and versioned inventory commands replace
client-authored transforms, health, and equipment. The source's working gameplay
is preserved; unused quest/XP paths remain dormant. Guest class selection,
respawn, bounded resources, and NPC health bars are explicit adaptations.

World distances multiply source units by worldScale=1/6; radians and seconds
remain unchanged. Source +Z facing maps to runtime yaw through
sourceYaw=pi-runtimeYaw. Terrain/scenery use fixed source seed-one and seed-two
noise. A bounded moving terrain patch and scenery window replace unbounded
client scenery accumulation. Our grass and sky are retained by request.

The earlier asset-selected local Paladin/zombie encounter remains separate from
the shared-world profile. It uses the source movement and combat data, adapted
collision/separation, a restart action, and one bounded health-bar draw.

Terrain simplex noise derives from Jonas Wagner's MIT implementation retained
by the source; see ../simplex-noise/LICENSE. Source art, character models, maps,
icons, sprites, scenery and equipment remain in private prepared asset directories
with their original notices. This repository contains no source or derived art.
See docs/quick-mmorpg-port.md for the feature inventory and verification scope.
